[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 703,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 466,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105527,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 483,
        "headingId": 466,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fibromatosis colli is a benign, self-limiting fibrous proliferation in the sternocleidomastoid muscle of infants. Some cases progress to muscle shortening, resulting in torticollis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:32:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 704,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 467,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105528,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 483,
        "headingId": 467,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:32:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 705,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 468,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105529,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 483,
        "headingId": 468,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>FB51.Y Other specified fibroblastic disorders</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:32:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 706,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 469,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105530,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 483,
        "headingId": 469,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> sternocleidomastoid tumour of infancy; pseudotumour of infancy; congenital muscular torticollis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:32:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 707,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 470,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105531,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 483,
        "headingId": 470,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:32:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 708,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 471,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105532,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 483,
        "headingId": 471,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fibromatosis colli affects the sternocleidomastoid muscle (the right more often than the left). Most commonly, the middle or distal third of the muscle is affected; about 15% of cases involve the entire muscle {7814599; 10356139}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:32:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 709,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 472,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105533,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 483,
        "headingId": 472,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fibromatosis colli manifests as a mass in the sternocleidomastoid muscle, most often congenitally or within 2&ndash;4&nbsp;weeks of birth. The mass may continue to grow over several weeks, then stabilize and spontaneously resolve in most cases {7814599; 10356139}. Approximately 10&ndash;20% of patients develop torticollis; such patients show head tilt towards the involved sternocleidomastoid muscle (chin directed towards the contralateral shoulder) {7814599}. Plagiocephaly may also occur. Fibromatosis colli may be associated with hip dysplasia, metatarsus adductus, talipes equinovarus, and prominent ears {7814599; 10356139}. Ultrasound is the preferred imaging method, demonstrating an isoechoic to hypoechoic, solid, well-defined ovoid mass in the muscle {9545475; 9719983}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:32:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 710,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 473,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105534,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 483,
        "headingId": 473,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Although uncommon, fibromatosis colli is the most common cause of a neck mass in the perinatal period. The reported incidence is 0.3&ndash;2.0% of live births {11379737}, with a male predominance (M:F&nbsp;ratio: 1.5:1) {7814599}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:32:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 711,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 474,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105535,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 483,
        "headingId": 474,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:32:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 712,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 475,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105536,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 483,
        "headingId": 475,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>A scar-like reactive process due to sternocleidomastoid muscle injury acquired in&nbsp;utero or during delivery is most likely {24205484; 8459000; 20091898}. Possible mechanisms include fetal malposition/crowding, birth trauma, vascular compromise, and infection {8459000}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:32:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 719,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 482,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105537,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 483,
        "headingId": 482,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The tumour is a firm to hard tan-white mass with associated skeletal muscle.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:32:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 713,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 476,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105538,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 483,
        "headingId": 476,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Surgical specimens are uncommon; only a minority of patients require tenotomy for persistent torticollis. Histology demonstrates a paucicellular, scar-like, fibroblastic proliferation entrapping skeletal muscle {20091898}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:32:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 714,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 477,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105539,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 483,
        "headingId": 477,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>FNAB may be used for diagnosis. Smears are hypocellular, composed of spindled fibroblasts associated with collagen fibres and multinucleated atrophic myocytes {2762289; 20091898; 11074630; 25584233}. The fibroblastic cells are singly dispersed or loosely cohesive, with bland oval to elongated nuclei and unipolar or bipolar cytoplasmic tails; bare nuclei may also be present {2762289; 12789914}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:32:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 715,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 478,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105540,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 483,
        "headingId": 478,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:32:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 718,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 481,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105541,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 483,
        "headingId": 481,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> a fusiform mass associated with the sternocleidomastoid muscle of an infant; biopsy or FNAB (if performed) shows a bland fibroblastic proliferation with associated atrophic skeletal muscle.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:32:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 716,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 479,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105542,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 483,
        "headingId": 479,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:32:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 717,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 480,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105543,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 483,
        "headingId": 480,
        "headingText": "<p>The majority of fibromatosis colli lesions are self-limiting, resolving within 6&nbsp;months. A subset of cases progress with tumour growth and/or the development of torticollis, which will worsen with patient growth if untreated {11379737}. Physiotherapy is effective in as many as 95% of cases. In the remaining cases, surgery may be required {10356139; 11379737; 20859860; 7814599}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-20T13:42:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]